A||N||18||Seanh||Views on Low Temp Stats||||3||
Z||000000||Seanh||11-30-2000||06:06 PM||||Just wondering what you think about the low temp stats for the car? Is it ok to let the car run at the lower temp? Maybe just a low temp stat and keep the fan switch stock, would that help any? Just wondering because I heard folks talking about how bad it is to let the car run at cool temps.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||3
Z||000001||mshill||11-30-2000||06:49 PM||||I run a 180 stat in the summer and a 195 in the fall and winter.  I have heard that it is probably OK to run a 180 but lower than that will likely approach the open loop mode of the ECM.  Also, in the last year I have torn down two V6 engines, one had 185K miles and the other 95K miles on it.  The 185K one had mostly highway miles on it (where it tends to get well warmed) and the 95K mile one was a short hop car.  The 185K motor was free of carbon inside the crankcase and heads, well oiled ad maintained.  The 95K mile motor was caked with carbon everywhere, it is like the engine never got warm enough to burn off the caked on oil so it just kept on building up.  I would be afraid that a 160 stat would not let the car warm up enough to burn off the carbon deposits.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||11-30-2000||07:00 PM||||The idea behind a low temp thermostat is that the intake manifold will be cooler, thus the intake air charge will be cooler. In reality, the effect is marginal at best. Going too cold with the thermostat (~160) will do exactly what mshill was talking about: it will not allow the oil to properly warm up and will reduce engine life. The sludge that normally sits at the bottom of the pan will be carried around the engine by the cold oil; and because it is thicker when cold, the oil will not properly lubricate all of the engine's internal parts.<BR>     The stock thermostat is fine for any performance application.<BR>     If you have an overheating problem, spend your money on hood louvers to let the air out from behind the radiator.<P>     BTW, when changing oil you can pour a couple of gallons of kerosene through the engine after the old oil has been drained but before the new oil goes in. The kerosene will go in the oil filler clear but come out the bottom black. It will gradually take more and more sludge out of the engine and get clearer and clearer. When it is about the color of tea you have probably cleaned the engine out as much as you can.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-30-2000||07:56 PM||||read the article in my cave <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Fiero Cave</A><P>Running a 180 won't cause any problems. It's the 160 that's trouble.<P>Ideally the fan should be on around the same temp as the thermostat. maybe a little warmer. This lets the car warm up quick but prevents the temp rising above thermostat value. (Stock fan turn on is 235F for the single speed fan.)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||11-30-2000||09:56 PM||||Oh, okay, I see now. I will probally use Rodneys set up in the summer, and change the stat to the stock one in winter. Would that be okay? Using the 180` stat in the summer and 195` in winter with a 205` on and 190` off fan switch?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||11-30-2000||10:00 PM||||&gt;Can you flush a motor with kerosene without<BR>&gt;worry that the small amount kerosene that<BR>&gt;will inevitably remain in motor might break<BR>&gt;down the new oil? Never heard of this; is<BR>&gt;it safe?<P>My father's been doing it a long time. If the car is in an attitude such that the drainplug is the lowest point in the oil pan, then all the kerosene will all drain out just like the oil. To do this on a (V6) fiero means that the car must be level or even have the front end higher than the rear. I've never worked on a 2M4.<BR>     The kerosene dissolves and removes sludge in the bottom of the oil pan that would otherwise remain there through the oil change and contaminate the new oil.<BR>     Obviously you don't want to start hte engine with kerosene in it. Just drain the old oil out, pour the kerosene through, put the drainplug back (don't forget this step!), change the filter and pour the new oil in.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||11-30-2000||10:08 PM||||Awesome. I am not really going to do this on my Fiero now, because I will not be changing the oil ever again in this engine. I will be starting the rebuild in about a month now, so I am just going to keep the oil thats in there, in there. I am however, going to do it to my truck. The oil return holes in the heads are plugged up, and I am going to drain the oil, fill with ATF, run it for 5 min. at idle and drain it out, then put some karosine in there and clean it out again. I was worried that it might melt gaskets or somthing. I had this happen once on a lawnmower, so I wasnt to sure. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tom88gt||11-30-2000||10:17 PM||||"motor flush" is basically repackaged and marked up kerosene.<P>As for the thermostats.  Engines are designed to operate within a particular tempature range.  The engineers at GM selected an appropriate thermostat to keep the engine within that temp range.  You can play with it a little but don't get extreme unless you have a modified engine.  Your engine was designed perform under a variety of conditions with the average driver (whatever that is).  You can tweek your stock engine a little for your particular unique driving style.  But for the most part, don't second guess the engineers who built your engine.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||11-30-2000||10:59 PM||||Seanh, the low temp switch with either thermostat is perfectly fine. with that setup the thermostat will be the main temp control, which is the way it should be.<P>as it is now the thermostat isn't the main control. The limiting control is the fan switch and at 235F that's way too far above the thermostat to properly regulate the temperature. With a 195 thermostat having the temp slowly swing up and down 20-30 degrees is real common. It's just not good for the car to do that.||||reg||1
Z||000009||webbee||11-30-2000||11:15 PM||||Seanh, that's the point of Marvel Mystery Oil. When you get the new engine in, after the rebuild, run a qt of MMO with your normal oil at every oil change. You won't have sludge in the engine. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||PlasticWrapd||12-01-2000||01:02 AM||||A little Kerosene left over in the oil shouldn't pose any problems.  As the engine reaches operating temperature, the kerosene will vaporize and be drawn out by the <BR>PCV valve.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Rodney||12-01-2000||08:44 AM||||Stant sells a 180 degree thermostat. 180 is a good temp to run at. I have 185-195 fan switches that work well with this thermostat. On my web page. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Wade87GT||12-01-2000||09:57 AM||||I've been using a 160 degree stat and a 176 degree fan switch for about two years now. I've had a noticable increase in power from the beginning. Other than it running a bit richer and using more gas, I haven't had any problems. I use Mobil 1 and it does turn black sooner than before. Is there something else I should be looking for before it's too late?||||reg||5
Z||000013||rogergarrison||12-01-2000||06:30 PM||||i run a 160* thermostat year round. and a 175* fan switch. my car has a 3.1 turbo and with a 180*,195* it wont run at all, sputters and spits with anything over half throttle. Runs perfect all the time, heat and defroster all work great in winter and runs way cooler in summer with traffic and AC. Ive never had it get over 1/2 way on gage<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Will||12-02-2000||12:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Seanh, the low temp switch with either thermostat is perfectly fine. with that setup the thermostat will be the main temp control, which is the way it should be.<P>as it is now the thermostat isn't the main control. The limiting control is the fan switch and at 235F that's way too far above the thermostat to properly regulate the temperature. With a 195 thermostat having the temp slowly swing up and down 20-30 degrees is real common. It's just not good for the car to do that.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I take issue with this statement. Exactly why is this "just not good for the car"? That does not compute. If a 30 degree temperature swing is not good, then you shouldn't run your engine more than 30 degrees above ambient air temp...<BR>     The thermostat isn't designed to rigidly control the engine's temperature. It's designed to keep it within a certain fairly broad range. The fan is purely a limiting device and running it continuously (especially when going down the highway at speed) is just a waste of power.<BR>     It's more work and expense, but put in hood louvres. They will augment the stock cooling system while allowing it to work the way it was designed.<P>I run the stock thermostat (V6) and in summers in standing traffic in Washington DC (without hood louvres) I don't have any overheating problems.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||12-02-2000||02:12 PM||||Hmmmm..... hood venting.... either way the system gets altered doesn't it. It just depends on wether you want to cut up your hood. That looks good on the GT's but I think it looks really strange on the others. Depending on which way it's done also becomes one more place for crap to collect in spring and fall. <P>My wording should probably have been differant above.... The thermostat sets the bottom limit the fan switch set the top. Having the fan on at or just above when the thermostat is full open keeps the engine closer to thermostat value which is what is the norm in the industry. Most cars keep coolant within 5-20 degrees above the thermostat, which means 200-215 when a 195 stat is in.<P>Having the temperature bouncing up and down between the thermostat and 235 where the stock fan comes on isn't good for it. It's both a performance issue and a reliability issue. And that's 235 coolant temp. The block is above that yet. You want to keep an engine in as narrow a range of temperature as posible. The ECM's programming is set for the enigine to give best performance in a pretty narrow range. It will run above or bellow that range but not as well.<P>Not all the Fiero's do this but enough do that it's an issue. My own 87 SC is fine as long as ambient air is below 80 but get much above that and the temp climbs pretty fast in heavy traffic. It starts knocking and pinging around 225, well before the fan comes on at 235 or more. Pinging will destroy an engine if left go. It will bounce up and down like a yoyo unless the fan is switched on by the AC. Even in cold weather the temperture climbs in traffic. It just does it allot slower.<P>having the fan come on with a lower temp switch won't hurt the fan a bit, and I doubt most people would notice the performance hit from increased alternator load. I'm not a big fan of the fixed switch in the block but it's allot simpler for many people to install.<P>Personally I am going to do this with an adjustable job on the radiator. The switch on the radiator can be set so that when the car is moving enough air to keep the thing cool the fan will shut off. As soon as you bog down in traffic the thing will heatup and start the fan again. This is how fan switches are done on allot of other cars and mini vans. Execpt they use a fixed switch threaded into the radiator outlet tank. (the bottom one on vertical flow, the left one in Fiero)<P>Another thing that gets forgotten with this is the transmision. The automatic tranny uses the radiator for cooling to. The hotter the rad the hotter the tranny and the shorter the fluid life. Don't just take my word for it. Here's a table from Borg-Warner. <A HREF="http://www.borg-warner.com/techtips/trans.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.borg-warner.com/techtips/trans.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||rodmcneill||12-02-2000||10:02 PM||||Hi guys you should check out the site that Theogre suggested. There is a lot of good info. there. I liked the one that told how to check coolant cond. by using a volt-ohm meter. I had never seen that before.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Seanh||12-02-2000||11:46 PM||||I am going to put the 180* stat in on the rebuilding and Rodneys fan switch also. I have found out, personally, that even when going down the highway, it will not generate enough air flow thru the rad. to cool it worth crap without the fan running. I am going to upgrade to a Permacool fan, and maybe a 4-core rad, still debating on that one. I know its a bit overkill for just a 3.4 engine, but I dont ever want to have any problems. I am going to be driving this car alot in 90*+ weather at the "big" lake here, with the A/C on and probally sitting for a while. So, I want no question about the cooling system. Thats a great site you said there. I never thought of testing coolant like that before. Thanks Guys||||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||12-04-2000||01:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><P>Hmmmm..... hood venting.... either way the system gets altered doesn't it.<P><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You can work around the problem or you can fix it.<BR>It just strikes me that hood vents are a more elegant and effective fix, especially the style of hood vents on (formerly) Archie's blue chop top and IMSA car.<BR>     It also seems to me that the stock system has shortcomings that are best remedied through hood vents. The main downfall of the stock system is that there is no natural place for the air to go after it exists the radiator. Turning the fan on doesn't help this problem. The air builds up a high pressure area behind the radiator, further impeding throughput. This HP area pushes air out under the car and at high speeds even pushes up the headlight doors. There are all kinds of ideas in this area, but most of them are addressing the wrong issue. Chris West's headlight door hold down wires keep the headlight doors down, but they don't fix the root of the problem: the HP area that's pushing the doors up in the first place. The most obvious, all encompassing solution is hood vents to relieve the pressure. Combine hood vents with a little sheet metal/fiberglass work under the radiator and you can channel ALL the air that goes through the radiator UP through the hood vents and OVER the car instead of DOWN and UNDER the car. This phenomenon should increase aerodynamic loading on the front end as well as reducing the airflow under the car; these two items should improve high speed stability. I haven't tested the high speed theory yet, but I am looking forward to the opportunity.<BR>     Learn a lesson from supercars. Ferrari is VERY much into positive ventilation, which aparently wasn't at the top of the priority list of Fiero engineers. <P>As for looking strange on coupes... I don't know about you, but I ALWAYS put functionality ahead of aesthetics.<P>||||reg||1
